# 15. Self-learning systems

## 1. Overview

In cybersecurity, self-learning systems use machine learning to continuously update their behavioral baselines. This helps detect stealthy threats like low-and-slow credential stuffing. Attackers increasingly use distributed, AI-driven botnets that rotate through thousands of IP addresses, making login attempts at a rate as low as one per minute per IP. Traditional API Gateway rate-limiting relies on static rules, such as blocking an IP if requests exceed a threshold, so it misses these slow attacks. An adaptive learning system can ingest and analyze streaming telemetry, including request headers, IP reputation, TLS fingerprints (like JA3/JA4), device context, and authentication failure patterns. The system analyzes this data and aggregates it at the campaign level to establish baselines of normal human interaction. This data allows administrators to build an adaptive API Gateway throttling algorithm that isolates zero-day anomalies and distributed botnets before attackers can steal credentials.

## 2. Thesis Statements

**Thesis Statement 1**
> A self-learning system in a cloud-native API Gateway can continuously analyze TLS and device fingerprints. This isolates and blocks low-and-slow credential stuffing attacks that evade static security rules.

**Thesis Statement 2**
> Reinforcement-learning-based adaptive API throttling continuously updates behavioral baselines to adjust access controls in real time. This prevents AI-driven botnets from testing stolen credentials while allowing legitimate users to log in.

## 3. Literature Review

**Literature Review supporting Thesis Statement 1**

Recent research on multi-layer fingerprinting shows that analyzing TLS fingerprints can isolate low-and-slow attacks.[^1] 

*   **Title:** *When Handshakes Tell the Truth: Detecting Web Bad Bots via TLS Fingerprints*
*   **Authors:** Ghalia Jarad, Kemal Bicakci
*   **Methodology and Findings:** The researchers tested whether protocol-level TLS fingerprinting (JA4) can distinguish bots from human users. They trained machine learning classifiers (XGBoost and CatBoost) on the JA4DB dataset. Automated tools use different underlying libraries than standard browsers, so their TLS `ClientHello` signatures are distinct. The study found this approach achieves high accuracy (AUC 0.998) in bot detection. This shows that TLS fingerprinting can identify credential stuffing scripts even when attackers rotate IP addresses or spoof `User-Agent` strings.
*   **Link:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09606
* [Link to Article](/15.3/1.1/2602.09606v1.pdf)

**Literature Review supporting Thesis Statement 2**

Frameworks using reinforcement learning (RL) show that self-learning algorithms can dynamically adjust API throttling.[^2][^3]

*   **Title:** *Multi-Objective Adaptive Rate Limiting in Microservices Using Deep Reinforcement Learning*
*   **Authors:** Ning Lyu, Yuxi Wang, Ziyu Cheng, Qingyuan Zhang, Feng Chen
*   **Methodology and Findings:** The authors proposed a deep RL strategy using DQN and A3C algorithms to adjust rate limits based on system load and traffic patterns. They replaced fixed static thresholds with an adaptive system that learns from continuous environmental feedback. The system improved throughput and reduced latency, showing that adaptive models can mitigate abuse while maintaining system stability and legitimate user access.
*   **Link:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.03279
* [Link to Article](/15.3/2.1/2511.03279v1.pdf)

*   **Title:** *Designing CAPTCHA Systems with Reinforcement Learning for Adaptive Defense*
*   **Authors:** Meghana Indukuri, Eman Naseerkhan, Joshua Rose, Martin Tran, Younghee Park
*   **Methodology and Findings:** This paper modeled bot detection as a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP) and used a PPO+LSTM agent to calibrate security challenges. The system dynamically triggers CAPTCHAs based on real-time classification confidence rather than static volumetric triggers. The findings showed that this adaptive defense mechanism addresses sophisticated bot threats, such as credential stuffing, by applying friction only when malicious intent is probable. This avoids negative impacts on legitimate users.
*   **Link:** https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/15/11/2363
* [Link to Article](/15.3/2.2/electronics-15-02363.pdf)

